  "narrative": {
    "essence": "Mexico is the host nation that can't quite escape its own ceiling — seven straight Round of 16 exits is a curse as much as a result. In 2026, playing on home soil with a nation watching, El Tri faces its defining moment: break the hex, or finally accept it.",
    "footballIdentity": "Football is Mexico's civic religion. Matches against the USA are wars. The Azteca roar is one of football's most intimidating sounds. But the hexatlon — six straight group-stage eliminations at the Round of 16 — has become the national wound, debated on every radio show and dinner table. Mexicans demand entertainment and expect drama. Their team delivers both.",
    "theMoment": "Mexico is co-hosting the World Cup for the third time — 1970, 1986, and now 2026. Estadio Azteca will host group stage matches, making it the only stadium to host three World Cups. The pressure is immense: a nation expects not just participation but redemption. After the R16 exits of 2018 and 2022, manager Javier Aguirre returned specifically to break the curse on the biggest stage Mexico has ever hosted.",
    "momentType": "footballing"
  },
  "keyPlayers": [
    {
      "name": "Raúl Jiménez",
      "club": "Fulham",
      "position": "Striker",
      "badge": null,
      "note": "The veteran fox in the box chosen to lead El Tri's attack over Santiago Giménez. Jiménez's movement, aerial ability, and hold-up play give Mexico a focal point — and at a home World Cup, this is the moment he's been building toward."
    },
    {
      "name": "Álvaro Fidalgo",
      "club": "Club América",
      "position": "Central mid",
      "badge": null,
      "note": "The Spanish-born naturalised Mexican who has become the creative heartbeat of El Tri. Fidalgo's range of passing and vision in tight spaces makes Mexico tick in possession."
    },
    {
      "name": "Brian Gutiérrez",
      "club": "Chicago Fire",
      "position": "Attacking mid",
      "badge": null,
      "note": "The youngest starter in today's XI and the most exciting Mexican talent in years. Gutiérrez's dribbling and directness give Mexico an unpredictability that they've lacked in recent tournaments."
    },
    {
      "name": "Julian Quiñones",
      "club": "Club América",
      "position": "Right wing",
      "badge": null,
      "note": "The Colombian-born winger who chose Mexico and has repaid the faith with direct running and goals. His pace on the right flank will be a constant threat."
    }
  ],
  "tactics": {
    "styleTags": [
      "Counter-attack",
      "Wing play",
      "Organized defense"
    ],
    "tacticalNote": "Aguirre sets Mexico in a compact 4-2-3-1 that invites pressure then releases Vega and Giménez on the break. Álvarez anchors the midfield, allowing the wide midfielders to press aggressively. Mexico looks to win the ball high on the left side and attack quickly through the right channel.",
    "strength": "Transition pace — Vega and Giménez can punish any team that overcommits, and Ochoa behind them buys time for the rest.",
    "vulnerability": "The same patterns that cost them in recent R16 exits: slow buildup, predictable wide deliveries, and a tendency to sit too deep once they take a lead."
  },
  "watchFor": [
    "Santiago Giménez's movement in the penalty area — he pulls center-backs wide and attacks the near post with runs from deep.",
    "Álvarez's positioning when Mexico defends — he effectively plays as a third center-back and dictates the defensive shape.",
    "How Mexico responds to falling behind — historically they struggle to chase games, relying too heavily on individual moments."
  ],
